# Super Smash runs with an empty environment. There is nothing to configure, no
# account to create, and no key to obtain — copy this file to `.env.local` only
# if you want to change one of the defaults below.
#
# That is not a simplification for the demo. The game has no backend: the
# simulation runs entirely in the browser, and online play is peer-to-peer over
# WebRTC with peer discovery handled by public infrastructure rather than by a
# server this project operates (DECISIONS D20). Vercel serves static files and
# nothing else.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- multiplayer

# The Trystero strategy used to find the other player before the direct WebRTC
# connection takes over. `torrent` uses public BitTorrent trackers and needs no
# account; `nostr` and `mqtt` are alternatives if a network blocks trackers.
#
# Whichever you pick only carries the handshake. Once the peers see each other,
# game traffic goes directly between them — and if you are on the same WiFi, ICE
# will discover and prefer the local path on its own, which is what makes
# same-room play feel instant.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SIGNAL_STRATEGY=torrent

# Namespaces the room codes, so two people typing "ABCD" on different builds do
# not land in the same match. Change it if you fork this and want your own
# rooms.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ROOM_PREFIX=super-smash

# Frames of input delay held back before rollback prediction takes over. Two is
# the default every shipping rollback game converges on: enough that a good
# connection almost never has to correct, small enough that nobody feels it.
# Raise it on a bad connection to trade a little input lag for fewer rollbacks.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_INPUT_DELAY=2
